DOOM for OS/2
=============

A port of the released DOOM source to OS/2 2.x and Warp, built with Open
Watcom 1.9.  The engine is id's, unchanged apart from the handful of places
listed under "Changes to the engine" below; everything OS/2 about it lives in
the platform modules.

Requires a real DOOM WAD, as every build of DOOM does.


Building
--------

    BUILD\MKOS2.CMD

This is a *cross build*.  It runs on a Windows machine with Open Watcom 1.9
installed -- Watcom's NT-hosted compiler emits OS/2 LX objects -- and produces
BUILD\DOOM.EXE for OS/2.  The result cannot be run on the machine that built
it.  Set %WATCOM% if Open Watcom is not at C:\WATCOM.

To build **on** OS/2 instead, there is a wmake makefile in the same
directory:

    cd BUILD
    wmake

Either way the object files stay in BUILD\, out of the source tree, so the
two never tread on each other's output.


Running
-------

Put DOOM.EXE next to a WAD, or point DOOMWADDIR at one, or name one outright:

    SET DOOMWADDIR=D:\GAMES\DOOM
    DOOM

    DOOM -iwad D:\GAMES\DOOM\DOOM.WAD

Run it from an OS/2 window, not a full screen session -- see "Why it is a VIO
program" below.  The configuration is written to DEFAULT.CFG beside the WADs.

Command line options, beyond the ones every DOOM has:

    -iwad <file>    Use this file as the game data, whatever it is called.
                    Also skips the "which game?" window when a directory
                    holds more than one.
    -2, -3          Open the window at two or three times its normal size.
                    The window can be resized freely afterwards regardless.
    -stretch        Let the window be any shape, rather than holding it to
                    4:3.  See "The window is 4:3" below.
    -nodive         Skip DIVE and use the GPI fallback.  Useful for telling
                    the two apart when something looks wrong.
    -divestretch    Let DIVE scale the picture, instead of scaling it here.
                    Faster where the display driver gets it right.  See
                    "Scaling is done in software" below.
    -noscale        Blit at 320x200 into the corner of the window, scaling
                    nothing.  A diagnostic, not a way to play.
    -noasm          Use the C versions of the two renderer inner loops
                    instead of the assembly ones.
    -nograbmouse    Leave the mouse pointer alone.  Without this the pointer
                    is captured and hidden whenever the window is active.
    -nosound        Do not open the audio device at all.
    -nomusic        Sound effects, but no music.
    -playlist       Try the MMPM/2 playlist interface on a card whose driver
                    has no DART support.  Off by default.  Read "Sound
                    effects need DART" below before using it.
    -sndformat <n>  Override the format assumed for a driver that will not
                    declare one.  0 is 11025 Hz 16 bit stereo, 3 is 11025 Hz
                    8 bit mono.  Only affects -playlist.
    -mb <n>         Zone heap size in megabytes.  Default 6.  DOS DOOM had
                    this; the Linux build did not.


Finding the game data
---------------------

The released code works out which game it is holding by the *name* of the
file, from a list of seven it knows.  That has two consequences, and both of
them bite.

An IWAD under any other name is not found at all: the game stops with
"W_InitFiles: no files found" and never says what it was looking for.

Worse, retail DOOM is only recognised as retail if the file is called
DOOMU.WAD.  Every real copy of it -- Ultimate DOOM, the Special Edition, the
Depths of DOOM boxes -- ships the file as DOOM.WAD, which matches the *next*
test down and is taken for the three-episode registered game.  It loads, it
plays, and Episode 4 is silently missing from the menu.

This port asks the file instead.  A WAD carries a directory of every lump in
it, and which maps are present says exactly which game it is: MAP01 means
DOOM II or a mission pack, E4M1 means retail, E2M1 means registered, and E1M1
alone means the shareware episode.  The name on the disk is still consulted
for the one thing it remains the best evidence for -- telling TNT and Plutonia
apart from DOOM II, which are identical in structure.

It looks in DOOMWADDIR, then the current directory, then the directory
DOOM.EXE is in.  That last one matters: a program started from the Workplace
Shell does not necessarily have its own directory as the current one, so a
DOOM.EXE sitting happily beside its WAD could otherwise still fail to find it.
It also takes any other *.WAD in those directories that turns out to be an
IWAD, and if there is genuinely nothing it says where it looked.

The identification is in D_OS2IWD.C and is the one part of this port that has
been tested directly: see "What has been tested" below.


When there is more than one game
--------------------------------

Keeping DOOM, DOOM II, TNT and Plutonia in one directory is the ordinary way
to have them, and the released code deals with that by taking the first name
off its list and playing that.  Which game starts is decided by a preference
order written in 1993, and the only way to overrule it is to rename files.

This port collects all of them instead of stopping at the first, and if there
is more than one it asks:

    More than one game was found.  Which one would you like to play?

      ( ) The Ultimate DOOM (4 episodes)          C:\GAMES\DOOM\DOOM.WAD
      (o) DOOM II: Hell on Earth                  C:\GAMES\DOOM\DOOM2.WAD
      ( ) Final DOOM: TNT Evilution               C:\GAMES\DOOM\TNT.WAD
      ( ) Final DOOM: The Plutonia Experiment     C:\GAMES\DOOM\PLUTONIA.WAD

The one already selected is whichever the old order would have started, so
pressing Enter keeps doing what this program has always done.  Arrow keys move
between them, a double click plays one outright, and Quit or the close box
exits without starting a game -- that is an answer, not an error, and it goes
quietly to the log rather than into a message box.

-iwad names a file outright and skips the question entirely, which is what to
put in a Workplace Shell object or a batch file for a machine that should
always start the same game.

One caveat on the names.  DOOM, the registered game, Ultimate DOOM and the
shareware episode are told apart by what is inside them and cannot be wrong.
DOOM II, TNT and Plutonia cannot: they are structurally identical -- MAP01 to
MAP32 and nothing to separate them -- so for those three the name comes from
the file name, and a TNT.WAD renamed to DOOM2.WAD will be offered as DOOM II.
That is why the window shows the full path beside every name: where the title
is a guess, the path is not.


How it works
------------

**Video.**  Two paths, chosen at startup and reported on the console.

  DIVE (Direct Interface Video Extensions) is the fast one.  DOOM's 320x200
  8-bit frame buffer is handed to DIVE, which blits it to the visible part of
  the window with the display driver doing the scaling and colour conversion.
  This is as close to the hardware as a PM program is allowed to get.

  GpiDrawBits is the fallback, and works on a plain OS/2 2.0 with no
  multimedia installed.  It goes through the whole graphics engine every
  frame, so it is slower, but it is correct.

  DOOM draws straight into the buffer the blitter reads -- I_InitGraphics
  points screens[0] at it -- so there is no copy between the renderer and the
  screen.  The X11 original did the same thing with the XImage's data.

**The window is 4:3.**  DOOM's picture is 320x200, but it was drawn to be
  seen on a 4:3 screen, where the pixels are half again as tall as they are
  wide.  Shown at 320x200 on square pixels everything comes out a fifth too
  short -- most obvious on the faces in the status bar and on anything round.
  So the window opens at 320x240 (times -2 or -3) and is held to 4:3 while it
  is dragged, and the blitter stretches the picture to fill it.

  The constraint is done by intercepting WM_ADJUSTWINDOWPOS on the frame,
  which changes the window and nothing else -- the blitter goes on filling
  whatever the client area turns out to be.  -stretch turns it off.

  Where the window was is remembered in DEFAULT.CFG and restored next time,
  clamped to the screen so that a position saved on a larger desktop cannot
  put the title bar somewhere it cannot be dragged back from.


**Scaling is done in software, not by DIVE.**

  DIVE will happily be told to stretch a 320x200 image into whatever the
  window is, and on a machine whose display driver does that correctly it is
  the best possible arrangement: the driver does the work, sometimes in
  hardware, and the game does nothing.

  It is not reliable.  On at least one 2.x era driver the stretch is wrong
  the moment the window is wider than the source: the picture comes out with
  content repeated to the left of where it belongs and walls appearing to
  change orientation as the view turns.  The same driver blits the same
  buffer perfectly at 1:1, which is what "-noscale" was added to demonstrate
  and what the fix relies on.

  So the picture is scaled here -- nearest neighbour, eight bits in and eight
  bits out, into a buffer the size of the client area -- and DIVE is asked
  only to copy it pixel for pixel.  Nearest neighbour rather than anything
  smoother because the values are palette indices: the average of two of them
  is a colour with no relation to either.

  It is only done when it is needed.  A client area exactly 320 pixels across
  needs no horizontal scaling, and vertical-only stretching is the case that
  works, so the default 320x240 window still costs nothing at all.  That
  matters: the default window is the one a 486 will be running.  Once a
  larger size has been asked for the buffer stays in use, rather than handing
  the source back and forth every time the window crosses 320.

  The scaling loop is written for the two cases that happen rather than the
  general one.  Vertical nearest neighbour repeats source rows -- 480 rows
  drawn from 200 means two or three of each -- so a row identical to the one
  above it is copied with memcpy rather than built again, and memcpy is a
  rep movsd.  A window exactly twice as wide as the picture, which is what
  the 640 option asks for, needs no column map at all: each source pixel
  becomes a pair, built in a register and stored in one go.  Together those
  are worth about three times the plain per-pixel loop, verified byte for
  byte against it at sixteen window sizes.  Rows are padded to a multiple of
  four bytes so neither shortcut ever straddles an alignment boundary; the
  padding is never blitted, because ulSrcWidth is the width in use and not
  the pitch.

  -divestretch goes back to letting DIVE scale, for a driver that does it
  properly and a machine that would rather spend the memory bandwidth
  elsewhere.

**Sound.**  MMPM/2 DART, at 11025 Hz 16-bit stereo, which is what DOOM's
  mixer produces.

  The mixing itself is id's, untouched.  What changed is who calls it.  On
  Linux the game loop mixed a buffer per rendered frame and wrote it to
  /dev/dsp; that only worked because the write blocked when the driver's
  buffer filled, and that blocking is what held the frame rate down to
  whatever the sound card was draining at.  Under DART, MMPM/2 calls back on
  a thread of its own each time a buffer has played, and the mixing happens
  there -- at exactly the rate the hardware consumes it, with the game loop
  left alone.  I_UpdateSound and I_SubmitSound are consequently empty.

  That second thread is the reason every module is compiled -bm.  The channel
  table that the callback reads and I_StartSound writes is held under a mutex
  semaphore.

**Music.**  The released source has none: the Linux build left every music
  entry point empty, and the DOS build's music code was never released -- it
  was the part that used the licensed sound library id could not ship.  So
  this is new, and it is in I_OS2MUS.C.

  DOOM stores its music as MUS, a compact relative of MIDI written for the
  DOS build, which nothing on OS/2 has heard of.  It is converted here into
  an ordinary type 0 MIDI file and handed to MMPM/2's MCI sequencer.

  The timing is exact rather than approximate: MUS counts in 140ths of a
  second, and a MIDI tick is (tempo / division) microseconds, so a division
  of 70 with the standard tempo of 500000 gives precisely 140 ticks a second
  with no rounding anywhere.

  Looping is done by asking MCI to notify the game window when the song ends
  and starting it again.  A PWAD that holds plain MIDI instead of MUS is
  passed through untouched.

**Networking.**  IBM TCP/IP, over the same UDP packets and the same port
  (5029) as every other DOOM, so this can play against linuxxdoom.

  Three things differ from Berkeley sockets and all three bite: sock_init()
  has to be called before anything works, soclose() closes a socket where
  close() does not, and errno says nothing -- sock_errno() does.

  SO32DLL and TCP32DLL are loaded by name, and only when -net is actually
  used.  A single player game on a machine with no TCP/IP never notices.

**No hard dependencies.**  DIVE, MMPM/2 and TCP/IP are all loaded by name at
run time rather than imported, so DOOM.EXE imports nothing but the base
system:

    DOSCALLS  NLS  KBDCALLS  VIOCALLS  PMWIN  PMGPI

It loads and plays on a bare OS/2 2.0, and picks up video acceleration, sound,
music and networking on any machine that has them.

  One trap worth recording, since it is invisible until you look: calling
  htons() or htonl() would have put an import on TCPIP32.DLL into the
  executable and undone the whole arrangement.  They are real functions in
  OS/2's library, not macros.  I_NET.C defines its own, which is what the
  Linux original did too -- "for some odd reason", as its comment has it.

**Behaving itself.**  Two things beyond the game proper.

  Alt-Tab away and DOOM stops burning a processor: it throttles to about
  twenty frames a second while the window is not active.  The game clock is
  deliberately left running, so the tics still pass at the right rate, and a
  network game is never slowed at all.

  If the game traps while it has the mouse pointer hidden, an exception
  handler registered in main() puts the pointer back before OS/2 gets on with
  its usual popup.  Hiding the pointer is a system-wide, reference-counted
  state; leaving the desktop without one over a bug in a game would be a
  rotten thing to do.


Why it is a VIO program
-----------------------

DOOM.EXE is linked "sys os2v2" -- a text-mode program -- and turns *itself*
into a Presentation Manager program when it opens its window, by changing its
own process type in its process information block.  I_OS2_MorphToPM does it.

This is a documented OS/2 facility and it exists for exactly this case.  A
program linked as PM from the start has nowhere to put text, and DOOM has a
great deal to say before its window opens: the whole "W_Init: Init WADfiles"
banner, and more to the point the message when a WAD cannot be found.
Morphing keeps that text in the OS/2 window the game was launched from and
still gets a PM window a moment later.

It does mean starting DOOM from an OS/2 *window*, not a full screen session.
If that is a problem, change one word in MKOS2.CMD -- "sys os2v2_pm" instead
of "sys os2v2" -- and it builds as a pure PM application with no source
change at all.  Everything printed before the window opens is then lost,
which is why I_Error also puts its message in a message box.

One thing to know if you do make that change: D_DoomMain calls getchar() to
make you press Enter after the "this version has been modified" banner when
-file is used.  In the VIO build that is fine.  As a pure PM binary it would
wait for ever with no visible prompt.


Changes to the engine
---------------------

The engine sources here are id's, and the diff against linuxdoom-1.10 is
deliberately small.  In full:

  d_main.c    An __OS2__ branch alongside the NORMALUNIX one for the WAD
              search paths, and a call into D_OS2FindIWAD before the
              filename tests.  Same DOOMWADDIR behaviour; what changes is
              that there is no $HOME to insist on -- OS/2 has no per-user
              home directory that is guaranteed to exist, and refusing to
              start without one would stop the game on an ordinary Warp
              desktop.  The configuration goes in DEFAULT.CFG beside the
              WADs.

              <direct.h> for mkdir, and the -cdrom call given the one
              argument OS/2's mkdir takes.

  w_wad.c     The unconditional "#define O_BINARY 0" is now guarded.  OS/2
              has a real text/binary distinction and <fcntl.h> already
              defines O_BINARY properly; flattening it to zero would stop
              every WAD read at the first 0x1a byte.

              strupr and filelength are no longer defined on OS/2 -- Watcom's
              run-time library has both, with different return types, and
              redefining them is a hard error.

  m_menu.c    O_BINARY on the savegame header read, for the same reason.

  m_misc.c    Four entries added to the defaults table for the window
              position, under __OS2__.

  m_bbox.h    <values.h> is a Unix header.  doomtype.h already defines MAXINT
              and MININT for every platform that is not Linux.

  r_data.c    alloca is declared in <malloc.h> on OS/2, not <alloca.h>.
              Without the prototype every stack buffer in that file arrives
              as a truncated pointer.

  doomdef.h   SNDSERV off.  There is no separate sound server process to
              fork and exec; the sound is integrated.

  i_main.c, i_system.c, i_video.c, i_sound.c, i_net.c
              Replaced entirely.  These are the port.

  os2doom.h   New: the PM handles, and the services more than one platform
              module needs.

  d_os2iwd.c  New: finding and identifying the IWAD.

  i_os2mus.c  New: MUS to MIDI, and the MCI sequencer.

Everything else is byte-identical to the release.


Sound effects need DART
-----------------------

Music and sound effects come from two different places, and only one of them
is fussy about the driver.

Music is MIDI.  The MUS score in the WAD is converted and handed to the MMPM/2
sequencer, which plays it on the card's synthesiser.  Every driver supports
this, and music works on hardware where nothing else does.

Sound effects are different: DOOM mixes them itself, sample by sample, and
needs somewhere to put a continuous stream of PCM.  The interface for that is
DART, and where DART exists this works well.  Where it does not, there is
nothing underneath it worth relying on.

The port can fall back to the older MMPM/2 playlist interface, and -playlist
asks it to.  It is off by default because on at least one real driver -- the
IBM Sound Blaster MCV driver, as used by the ReSound New Wave -- it stops the
whole machine.  Not a trap in DOOM: a hard lock, needing the power switch,
with a file system check to follow.

That was chased a long way.  Four separate faults were found and fixed on the
way to it, every one of them real: memory handed to a 16-bit driver without a
16:16 alias, a stream restart that raced the DMA engine, a mixing backlog that
pegged the processor, and an eight bit mono assumption made about a device
that was playing sixteen bit stereo.  With all four corrected -- the format
measured rather than guessed, the mixer producing a healthy signal, and the
playlist stripped to nothing but the buffers and the loop -- the machine still
stopped, and ran perfectly the moment -playlist was left off.

Then the shape of the failure was pinned down, or so it looked.  A playlist
ending in EXIT_OPERATION plays its blocks once and stops, and that survives;
the same list ending in BRANCH_OPERATION, looping, does not.  The obvious
reading was that looping is the problem, so the code was changed to play one
complete pass at a time and start the next when the device reported the last
one finished -- no looping anywhere, every play ending properly.

That locked the machine harder than anything before it: Ctrl-Alt-Del would not
answer, the power switch was the only way out, and the file system needed
repair afterwards.

So the reading was wrong, and it is worth writing down why, because it is the
kind of mistake that is easy to repeat.  The EXIT test did not isolate looping.
It isolated a *small, bounded amount of playback* -- four blocks, a third of a
second, and then nothing.  Both of the arrangements that fail are ones that
keep the device playing indefinitely; the one that survives is the one that
barely plays at all.  The common ingredient is sustained streaming itself, not
the opcode used to sustain it.

That makes it final.  Every way of streaming audio comes down to feeding a
device continuously, and this driver cannot be fed continuously by any route
it will accept.  There is no restructuring left to try, because the failing
ingredient is the thing all the structures have in common.

This driver has refused every interface it was offered: no DART, no MCI_SET of
any wave parameter, no MCI_OPEN_ELEMENT.  A driver that says no to all of
those and yes to this one was probably never going to mean it.

If your card has a DART-capable driver, none of this applies and sound effects
work without any switch at all.

If it does not, then on this hardware -playlist is not a switch worth trying.
It has cost five reboots, one of them a hard power-off with file system damage
following.  It is kept because a different driver may well handle it properly,
not because it is expected to work here.  If you try it anyway: nothing else
running, nothing unsaved, and be ready to reach for the power switch.


What has been tested
--------------------

The parts that could be tested without an OS/2 machine, have been.  Both of
these compile the real module -- not a copy of it -- against a small shim and
run it on the build host:

  * IWAD identification, against synthetic WADs built for the purpose.  Nine
    checks: each game mode from its lumps, TNT and Plutonia by name, a retail
    IWAD named DOOM.WAD correctly identified as retail rather than
    registered, and PWADs, map-less IWADs and missing files all refused.

  * MUS to MIDI conversion, against scores built note by note.  Ten checks:
    note on and off, volume carried over when the byte is omitted, the
    channel 9/15 percussion swap in both directions, program changes,
    controller mapping, pitch bend centring on 8192, multi-byte variable
    length delays, refusal of non-MUS data, and the shape of the finished
    file.

The executable itself has been verified to be a proper LX binary for the
80386 under OS/2, flagged windowable-VIO, importing only the six base DLLs
listed above.  The whole tree compiles clean at -w3.


What has not been tested
------------------------

Everything that needs the machine.  The things most likely to want attention,
in the order I would check them:

  1. The DIVE image orientation.  SETUP_BLITTER's fInvert is FALSE, which
     says the source is stored top-down -- which is how DOOM has always held
     its frame buffer.  If the picture comes out upside down on some display
     driver, that flag is the one to turn over.  It is commented in
     I_VIDEO.C.

  2. The keyboard.  Keys are read from PM's scan codes, because those are the
     only thing that reports shift, control and alt going up as well as down
     -- and DOOM uses all three.  The arrow keys and the grey navigation
     block send an 0xe0 prefix on the wire, and how much of that survives
     into WM_CHAR's scan code byte has varied between OS/2 keyboard drivers.
     I_TranslateKey falls back to the virtual key and then the character
     code, so it should cope either way, but this is the part most worth
     watching.

  3. The music.  The conversion is tested; what is not is whether MMPM/2's
     sequencer is happy being handed a file and told to loop, and whether the
     MCI_NOTIFY message arrives as expected.  If music plays once and stops,
     that is where to look.  -nomusic turns it off.

  4. DART buffer sizes.  MMPM/2 is free to hand back a different buffer size
     from the one asked for.  The callback fills whole mixes and refuses to
     start if the buffer is too small to hold even one, and the size actually
     given is printed at startup.

  5. The socket entry point names in SO32DLL and TCP32DLL.  If one is wrong,
     -net fails at startup with a clear message and single player is
     unaffected.


The icon
--------

RES\DOOM.ICO is an ordinary Windows icon, and it is converted at build time.

Do not feed wrc a Windows .ICO for an OS/2 target.  It accepts it, exits 0,
and embeds the ICONDIR and Windows BITMAPINFOHEADER verbatim as an RT_POINTER;
PM then cannot load it, and you get no icon and no error anywhere to say why.

So MKOS2.CMD builds TOOLS\ICO2OS2.C for the build machine -- it is a host
program, compiled -bt=nt like anything else that runs on Windows -- and runs
it over RES\DOOM.ICO to produce BUILD\DOOM.OS2, which is what RES\DOOM.RC
compiles in.  The pixels are unchanged; only the wrapping around them differs.

What PM wants is a bitmap array holding one device form, and that form holds
TWO version-1 'CI' bitmaps: the mono AND/XOR mask first at double height, then
the colour bitmap, each followed immediately by its own colour table.  Three
details are easy to get wrong and all three are fatal rather than merely ugly
-- a colour icon is not two array entries, the version-1 structures are the
ones IBM's own icons use rather than the documented "2" long forms, and a
version-1 colour table is 3-byte RGB, so a Windows RGBQUAD's padding byte has
to be dropped.  The layout in ICO2OS2.C was established by parsing a real Warp
3 icon field by field, not from the published description; the comment at the
top of the file records it.

The whole step is optional and skips itself.  With no RES\DOOM.ICO the build
prints a line saying so and links exactly as it otherwise would.  A genuine
OS/2 icon can be used instead by dropping it into BUILD\ as DOOM.OS2 by hand.

The icon is loaded with WinLoadPointer and applied with WM_SETICON rather
than asked for through FCF_ICON in the frame flags.  That is deliberate: PM
validates an icon resource strictly, and with FCF_ICON a resource it dislikes
fails the whole WinCreateStdWindow with PMERR_INVALID_RESOURCE_FORMAT and no
window at all.  Done this way the worst a bad icon can do is leave the
default one in place.


